<article class="article">
  <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/blog">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Negative review templates</div>
  <div class="tag">Templates</div>
  <h1>How to respond to negative Google reviews: templates for 12 situations</h1>
  <div class="meta">Updated 9 October 2026. Google's help pages and the rules cited here were checked on that date.</div>

  <p>Each template below fits one situation: a long wait, a surprise charge, a review you think is fake, a patient complaint. Each one also says what to leave out, because a few kinds of reply break Google's review policies or, in healthcare, privacy law. Replace everything in [brackets] and read the reply aloud before you post it.</p>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#structure">The five parts of a reply</a>
    <a href="#templates">12 templates</a>
    <a href="#industry">By industry</a>
    <a href="#never">What never to write</a>
    <a href="#after">After you reply</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="structure">The five parts Google's own tips point to</h2>
  <p>Google publishes its own advice for replying to negative reviews in Business Profile Help. Every template on this page follows it.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data stack">
    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What Google's guidance says</th><th>In the reply</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td data-label="Part">1. Their name and their point</td><td data-label="What Google's guidance says">&ldquo;Address the reviewer by name and acknowledge their specific feedback.&rdquo;</td><td data-label="In the reply">&ldquo;Hi Dana, a 40-minute wait with a booking is too long.&rdquo;</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Part">2. An apology for what went wrong</td><td data-label="What Google's guidance says">&ldquo;Apologize when appropriate&rdquo;, and &ldquo;don't take responsibility for things outside your control.&rdquo;</td><td data-label="In the reply">Say sorry once, for the specific thing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Part">3. What you can and cannot do</td><td data-label="What Google's guidance says">&ldquo;Explain what you can and cannot do in that situation.&rdquo;</td><td data-label="In the reply">&ldquo;We now text guests when a table is running late.&rdquo;</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Part">4. A private channel for the details</td><td data-label="What Google's guidance says">&ldquo;politely request that the reviewer contact you via phone or email to resolve the issue privately.&rdquo;</td><td data-label="In the reply">A direct phone line or email, not a general inbox.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Part">5. Your name</td><td data-label="What Google's guidance says">&ldquo;A personal sign-off with your name or initials shows that their experience matters to you.&rdquo;</td><td data-label="In the reply">&ldquo;Marco, owner&rdquo;</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Google adds two more: &ldquo;Keep it short and simple&rdquo; and &ldquo;Respond in a timely manner.&rdquo; Source: <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/3474122#review-reply-tips" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Business Profile Help, tips to write better review replies</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="templates">Templates by situation</h2>

  <h3 id="t1">1. A long wait or slow service</h3>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>Hi [first name], thank you for telling us about [day]. Waiting [length of wait] for [your table / your food] is too long, and someone should have kept you updated. Since then we've [the change, for example started telling guests at the door when the kitchen is running behind]. If you'd like to talk it through, my direct line is [phone]. [Your name], [role]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> &ldquo;We were short-staffed&rdquo; on its own. An explanation with no change attached reads as an excuse.</p>

  <h3 id="t2">2. A wrong, poor or faulty order</h3>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>Hi [first name], I'm sorry your [dish / product] wasn't right. [A cold main course / A cracked case] isn't what you paid for. We've [checked the batch / gone through the dish with the kitchen]. Please send your order details to [direct email] and I'll sort it out with you directly. [Your name]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> a public refund or free-meal offer. Google asks for replies that are &ldquo;conversational, not promotional&rdquo; and says to &ldquo;avoid using your response to offer deals or promotions.&rdquo; Make any goodwill gesture in private, and never tie it to the review.</p>

  <h3 id="t3">3. Rude or unhelpful staff</h3>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>Hi [first name], thank you for raising this, and I'm sorry. Nobody should be spoken to like that at [business name]. I've taken it up with the team. If you can send me the date and time at [direct email], I'll look into it myself. [Your name], [role]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> the employee's name and anything about warnings or dismissals. Google's personal information policy covers posting someone's full or last name without their consent, and a public reply is the wrong place to discuss a member of staff.</p>

  <h3 id="t4">4. A bill, price or unexpected charge</h3>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>Hi [first name], I can see why the [charge] was a surprise. [One-sentence explanation, for example: weekend call-outs carry a fee, and it is listed on the quote.] If that isn't what you were told, send your invoice number to [direct email] and I'll check it by [day]. [Your name]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> their invoice total, card details or payment history. Google's personal information policy names financial information.</p>

  <h3 id="t5">5. You were late or didn't turn up</h3>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>Hi [first name], you're right: we gave you a [time] slot and [arrived at 2pm / didn't arrive]. I'm sorry you lost [a morning / a day off] waiting for us. We now [confirm arrival windows by text the evening before]. I'd like to rebook at a time that suits you; please call me on [direct number]. [Your name]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> a long account of the previous job or the traffic. One clause is enough, and only if it is true.</p>

  <h3 id="t6">6. The result wasn't what they wanted</h3>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>Hi [first name], I'm sorry you weren't happy with your [haircut / repair / clean]. We should have checked the result with you before you left. Please contact [name] on [direct number] so we can look at it together and agree how to put it right. [Your name]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> a public argument about whether the result matched the request. If you disagree, have that conversation privately.</p>

  <h3 id="t7">7. A policy they disagree with</h3>
  <p>Deposits, cancellation fees and return windows. Google's tip here is to &ldquo;be honest and explain limitations.&rdquo;</p>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>Hi [first name], I understand the [cancellation fee] was frustrating. We charge it because [honest reason, for example: a missed slot can't be rebooked at short notice], and it is shown [at booking and in the confirmation email]. We apply it to every booking, but I'm happy to go through yours with you at [direct email]. [Your name]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> their booking details, quoted back to them in public.</p>

  <h3 id="t8">8. Something outside your control</h3>
  <p>Weather, a delivery app, a supplier, a road closure. Google's own example is an event cancelled because of bad weather.</p>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>Hi [first name], I'm sorry your order arrived [late and cold]. Deliveries through [app name] are carried by the app's own drivers, so we can't track them once they leave us. What we can do is check when it left our kitchen: send your order number to [direct email] and I'll look into it. [Your name]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> sharp words about the other company. Google's advice is to &ldquo;acknowledge if any mistakes were made, but don't take responsibility for things outside your control.&rdquo;</p>

  <h3 id="t9">9. A review you can't match to any customer</h3>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>Hi [first name], thank you for the feedback. We can't find a [booking / order] that matches, so we can't look into it yet. Could you contact [name] at [direct email] with the date you visited? We'd like to understand what happened. [Your name]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> &ldquo;You were never here.&rdquo; Your records may be incomplete, and readers take it as an accusation.</p>

  <h3 id="t10">10. A review you believe is fake, from a competitor or a former employee</h3>
  <p>Report it first (the steps are under <a href="#after">After you reply</a>). Google's policy does not allow reviews based on a conflict of interest, which Google says may include current or former employment and industry competitors. While Google looks at it, reply neutrally or not at all.</p>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>We have no record of this [visit / order] and have asked Google to review it. If we've got this wrong, please contact [name] at [direct email] and we'll look into it. [Business name]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> accusations. Google's content policy lists &ldquo;unsubstantiated allegations of unethical behavior or criminal wrongdoing&rdquo; as offensive content, and replies must follow the same policies. In the US, the FTC's rule on consumer reviews (16 CFR 465.7) bans using an unfounded legal threat, intimidation, or a public accusation made knowing it is false (or with reckless disregard for the truth), in response to a review, to get it removed.</p>

  <h3 id="t11">11. A run of 1-star reviews followed by a demand for money</h3>
  <p>This is review extortion, and Google has a separate report form for it. Google's instructions: &ldquo;Do not engage with or pay the malicious individuals&rdquo; and &ldquo;Do not try to resolve it yourself by offering money or services.&rdquo; Keep screenshots of every demand showing the date, time and sender, collect links to the reviews, then submit the <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/16404809" target="_blank" rel="noopener">merchant extortion report</a>. You do not need to reply to each review. If you want one line under each for customers reading:</p>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>We have no record of this visit and have reported this review to Google. [Business name]</p>
  </div>

  <h3 id="t12">12. A patient complaint</h3>
  <p>For dentists, doctors, therapists and other healthcare providers, the reply must not reveal anything about anyone's care, including whether the reviewer is a patient.</p>
  <div class="template">
    <div class="template-label">Template</div>
    <p>Thank you for taking the time to write. Privacy rules mean we can't discuss anyone's care in a public reply, including whether they are a patient here. We would like to hear more: please call our practice manager, [name], on [number]. [Practice name]</p>
  </div>
  <p class="leave-out"><strong>Leave out:</strong> names, appointment dates, treatments, costs, insurance, and &ldquo;we checked your records&rdquo;. In the US this has cost practices money: an HHS Office for Civil Rights investigation indicated that Manasa Health Center, a New Jersey psychiatric practice, disclosed the health information of four patients in replies to their negative Google reviews; the practice agreed to pay $30,000 in 2023. New Vision Dental in California paid $23,000 in 2022 over disclosures in replies to online reviews. In the UK, health information is special category data under UK GDPR.</p>

  <h2 id="industry">Which template to start from, by industry</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data stack">
    <thead><tr><th>Industry</th><th>What changes in the reply</th><th>Start from</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td data-label="Industry">Restaurants and caf&eacute;s</td><td data-label="What changes in the reply">Name the dish and the day. Ask for order details privately.</td><td data-label="Start from">1, 2, 8</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Industry">Hotels and rentals</td><td data-label="What changes in the reply">Keep guest names, stay dates and room numbers out of the reply.</td><td data-label="Start from">1, 6, 7</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Industry">Dentists, clinics, therapists</td><td data-label="What changes in the reply">Never confirm that someone is a patient.</td><td data-label="Start from">12</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Industry">Trades and home services</td><td data-label="What changes in the reply">Arrival windows and quotes. Offer to rebook by phone.</td><td data-label="Start from">4, 5, 6</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Industry">Salons and beauty</td><td data-label="What changes in the reply">Invite them to look at the result with you, privately.</td><td data-label="Start from">3, 6, 7</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Industry">Shops and online stores</td><td data-label="What changes in the reply">Keep order numbers private. Explain the carrier once.</td><td data-label="Start from">2, 4, 8</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>For full replies to positive and mixed reviews in these industries, see <a href="/blog/google-review-response-examples">Google review response examples</a> and the <a href="/blog/google-review-reply-examples-restaurants">restaurant examples</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="never">What never to write, and why</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table class="data stack">
    <thead><tr><th>Don't write</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td data-label="Don't write">&ldquo;We'll give you [anything] if you update or remove your review.&rdquo;</td><td data-label="Why">Google does not allow merchants to offer incentives &ldquo;in exchange for posting any review or revision or removal of a negative review.&rdquo;</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Don't write">&ldquo;Come back for 20% off.&rdquo;</td><td data-label="Why">Google's reply tips: &ldquo;avoid using your response to offer deals or promotions.&rdquo;</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Don't write">&ldquo;You were never here&rdquo; or &ldquo;This is a competitor&rdquo;, without proof</td><td data-label="Why">Google lists unsubstantiated allegations as offensive content. In the US, knowingly false public accusations made to get a review removed are banned by 16 CFR 465.7.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Don't write">&ldquo;Our lawyers will be in touch.&rdquo;</td><td data-label="Why">16 CFR 465.7 also bans unfounded legal threats and intimidation used to prevent or remove a review.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Don't write">A patient's name, visit, treatment or insurance</td><td data-label="Why">HIPAA. See the HHS settlements under template 12.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Don't write">A reviewer's or employee's full name, phone, booking or order details</td><td data-label="Why">Google's personal information policy. Your own business phone and email are allowed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Don't write">The same paragraph under every review</td><td data-label="Why">Google: &ldquo;Instead of sending the same &lsquo;thank you&rsquo; to everyone, focus on reviews where you can share a helpful update or answer a question.&rdquo; In BrightLocal's 2026 survey, generic or templated replies put off 50% of consumers.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2 id="after">After you reply</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Google checks your reply first.</strong> Google says replies usually take up to 10 minutes to review, sometimes up to 30 days. If a reply is not approved, you are asked to edit it.</li>
    <li><strong>The reviewer is notified.</strong> They can still change their review after reading your reply, and the review's date then updates.</li>
    <li><strong>If the review breaks Google's policies, report it.</strong> In your Business Profile, select Read reviews, then Report next to the review, choose a reason and send. Google says evaluation typically takes several days; you can follow the status, and appeal once, in the Reviews Management Tool.</li>
    <li><strong>A review you simply disagree with stays.</strong> Google's words: &ldquo;Do not report a review just because you disagree with it or dislike it. Google doesn't get involved in conflict between businesses and customers.&rdquo;</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="cta">
    <h2>Draft the reply, then add what only you know</h2>
    <p>Paste the review into the negative review reply generator and choose the Empathetic tone. Add your fix, a direct contact and your name before you post.</p>
    <a href="/negative-reviews#tool">Write a reply to a negative review</a>
  </div>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <h3>Should I reply to a negative review I think is fake?</h3>
    <p>Report it first: in your Business Profile, open Read reviews, select Report next to the review and choose a reason. Then reply neutrally or not at all. Do not accuse the reviewer in public; Google treats unsubstantiated allegations as offensive content, and replies must follow the same policies.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <h3>Can I offer a refund or a free visit in my reply?</h3>
    <p>Offer to talk privately instead. Google asks owners to avoid deals or promotions in replies, and offering anything in exchange for changing or removing a review breaks Google's policy. If a refund is fair, arrange it privately and never tie it to the review.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <h3>Can I ask the customer to change their review?</h3>
    <p>Google notes that customers can change their review after reading your reply. You cannot offer anything in exchange for a change or removal. Put the problem right first; whether to update the review is the customer's decision.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <h3>How quickly should I reply to a negative review?</h3>
    <p>Google's tip is to respond in a timely manner. In BrightLocal's 2026 survey of 1,002 US adults, 32% expected a reply by the next day and 81% within a week.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq" style="border:none">
    <h3>How long should a reply to a negative review be?</h3>
    <p>Google's advice is to keep it short and simple. Three to five sentences is enough for the five parts: their point, an apology where it is due, what you did or cannot do, a direct contact and your name.</p>
  </div>

